id before if you have any  questions just Chuck them in the chat  and we'll answer them on the fly or as  we go  okay so let's Dive In  so  um just for those who maybe are new to  this field of future learning  um there's like many different kind of  definitions of future learning in in the  literature but I think the most famous  One  um really comes from the gpt3 paper by  openai  and in that case or in that paper what  they were  um sort of showing  is that when you do language modeling so  when you do pre-training at scale a  large part of the Corpus includes  instructions where you might for example  see something like translate English to  French some of these examples here and  then what you can do is you can get the  model to kind of complete  um those examples and then it kind of  implicitly learns how to do for example  translation even though it's never  explicitly trained on a translation task  and the reason it's called fuchsia is  because you provide a few of these kind  of examples to sort of prime the model  at inference time  um and in the case of like large  language models like gpt3 this is  typically called in context learning  because you're basically doing this at  inference time based on the context  and the in the original gpt3 paper they  showed that if you look at the kind of  accuracy over many different benchmarks  in NLP you see this kind of you know  gradual Trend as you scale the number of  parameters in in the model and so for  example the the line here that we're  interested in is this orange line for  the fuchsia  and so you can see that basically you  know when the language model is at the  scale of like say like I don't know Bert  or a bit larger your performance or your  average accuracy is around 35  but as you scale up to like the 175  billion parameters of gpd3 you you  roughly you know  um kind of double that or let's say one  and a half times that average accuracy  so this kind of suggests like a  promising uh way of thinkin